---
layout: base.njk
title: "Insights"
summary: "Articles and updates from Natural Trace on molecular traceability, supply chain authenticity, and the natural products industry."
pagination:
  data: collections.insightsRegular
  size: 10
  alias: posts
permalink: "{% if pagination.pageNumber == 0 %}/insights/{% else %}/insights/page/{{ pagination.pageNumber + 1 }}/{% endif %}"
---
{#-
  Rows, ten to a page, with a numbered pager.

  The row is CNA's (channelnewsasia.com/topic/insight), which Bryan asked for
  on 1 September 2026 after the card layout was measured at roughly 700px per
  article: a small picture, a category kicker, the headline, a date, and no
  summary. That rebuild also dropped the pager and put the whole archive on
  one page, reasoning that twenty rows was only 2,700px of scroll.

  The pager is back on 3 September 2026, at Bryan's request, with a page
  selector. One page of everything read as the articles being squeezed
  together, and he wanted them broken into pages a reader can pick from. Ten
  a page is about 1,500px at the row pitch, a screen and a half rather than a
  scroll, and the archive is currently two pages. Numbered links rather than
  "load more", so every page is a real URL that can be linked, bookmarked and
  indexed, and 2022 is one click away.

  What that changed around it:

  - The topic control no longer filters this page; it goes to the tag archive.
    A show/hide filter only ever sees the rows on the page it is on, so on a
    paginated list it would say "2 articles in Food Fraud" while the third sat
    on page two. Every topic now has an archive (TAG_PAGE_MIN in .eleventy.js
    is 1, and the note there says why) and the control is one partial on the
    index and the archives both: partials/insights-topic-select.njk.
  - The pinned introduction sits after the last regular row on the last page,
    which is where an introduction to an archive belongs.
  - The LinkedIn strip is on the first page only.
  - The redirects that sent /insights/page/2/ and /3/ to the index while the
    pager was gone are deleted: a redirect stub and a real page cannot both
    write the same file, and docs/rollback-v3.md said to remove them in the
    same commit as the pager.

  The summary is still absent from the index. That is unchanged and is a
  content decision rather than a layout one; docs/rollback-v3.md has the two
  lines that put it back.
-#}

<section class="insights-listing">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="insights-header">
      <span class="section-label">Insights</span>
      <h1>News &amp; Updates{% if pagination.pageNumber > 0 %} <span class="insights-page-of">page {{ pagination.pageNumber + 1 }}</span>{% endif %}</h1>
      <p class="insights-subtitle">The latest from Natural Trace: product developments, industry perspectives and company news.</p>
    </div>

    {% set total = collections.insightsRegular.length + collections.insightsPinned.length %}
    {% if total > 0 %}
    {%- set lastPage = not pagination.href.next %}
    {%- set shownHere = posts.length + (collections.insightsPinned.length if lastPage else 0) %}
    {%- set firstIndex = pagination.pageNumber * pagination.size + 1 %}
    {%- set lastIndex = firstIndex + shownHere - 1 %}

    {% set currentTopic = "" %}
    {% include "partials/insights-topic-select.njk" %}

    {#- CNA's "Showing 15 of 166", in full this time. With the archive across
        pages the first half is a real answer: it says where this page sits in
        the whole, which is what a reader deciding whether to press Older
        wants to know. -#}
    <p class="insights-count">Showing {{ firstIndex }}&ndash;{{ lastIndex }} of {{ total }} article{% if total != 1 %}s{% endif %}</p>

    <div class="insights-rows">
      {% for post in posts %}
        {% include "partials/insight-row.njk" %}
      {% endfor %}

      {#- The evergreen introduction, last on the last page. An introduction
          to an archive belongs at the end of it, not above this month's news,
          and on a paginated list the end is the foot of the final page. -#}
      {% if lastPage %}
      {% for post in collections.insightsPinned %}
        {% include "partials/insight-row.njk" %}
      {% endfor %}
      {% endif %}
    </div>

    {% if pagination.pages.length > 1 %}
    {#- Newer / Older rather than Previous / Next, because the list is in date
        order and that is the direction a reader is actually moving in. The
        step at either end stays in the layout as an inert span so the numbers
        do not shift sideways between the first page and the second. -#}
    <nav class="insights-pager" aria-label="Insights pages">
      {% if pagination.href.previous %}
      <a class="insights-pager-step" href="{{ pagination.href.previous }}" rel="prev">
        <span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Newer
      </a>
      {% else %}
      <span class="insights-pager-step is-off"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Newer</span>
      {% endif %}

      <ol class="insights-pager-numbers">
        {% for entry in pagination.pages %}
        <li>
          {% if loop.index0 == pagination.pageNumber %}
          <span class="insights-pager-num is-current" aria-current="page">{{ loop.index }}</span>
          {% else %}
          <a class="insights-pager-num" href="{{ pagination.hrefs[loop.index0] }}"
             aria-label="Page {{ loop.index }}">{{ loop.index }}</a>
          {% endif %}
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ol>

      {% if pagination.href.next %}
      <a class="insights-pager-step" href="{{ pagination.href.next }}" rel="next">
        Older <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
      {% else %}
      <span class="insights-pager-step is-off">Older <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span>
      {% endif %}
    </nav>
    {% endif %}

    {% else %}
    <div class="insights-empty">
      <h2>Coming Soon</h2>
      <p>We're preparing our first insights. Check back soon for news, updates, and industry perspectives from the Natural Trace team.</p>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</section>

{#- Posts that were only ever going to be LinkedIn posts.
    Content comes from src/_data/linkedin.yml, edited under "From our LinkedIn"
    in the CMS. Nothing is fetched from LinkedIn at build time: an editor
    chooses a post and pastes its address. LinkedIn publishes no feed of a
    company page's posts, and the API that would answer for one needs an app
    review and a key that expires every sixty days, which is not a thing to put
    in a build that four people share.

    Below the articles and the pager, not above them. The articles are the
    version of record (docs/publishing-to-linkedin.md sets out why) and this is
    an aside to them. First page only: an aside that repeated on every page
    would stop reading as an aside.

    A button rather than the post itself, which is the part most likely to look
    like an oversight later. Loading the frame loads a LinkedIn page, and that
    sets LinkedIn's cookies for every visitor who reaches this page, whatever
    they answered in the consent bar. The bar covers our own analytics and says
    so; stretching that answer to cover somebody else's tracking would make the
    wording untrue. So the frame waits to be asked, and the asking is the
    consent. With JavaScript off, or if main.js fails to load, the link below
    the button still goes to the post. -#}
{% if pagination.pageNumber == 0 and linkedin.posts and linkedin.posts.length > 0 %}
<section class="linkedin-strip">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="insights-header">
      <span class="section-label">LinkedIn</span>
      <h2>{{ linkedin.heading }}</h2>
      {% if linkedin.subheading %}<p class="insights-subtitle">{{ linkedin.subheading }}</p>{% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="linkedin-grid">
      {% for post in linkedin.posts %}
      <div class="linkedin-embed"
           data-embed-src="{{ post.url | linkedinEmbed }}"
           data-embed-title="LinkedIn post: {{ post.title }}">
        <p class="linkedin-embed-label">{{ post.title }}</p>
        <button type="button" class="linkedin-embed-load">Show this post</button>
        <p class="linkedin-embed-note">
          Loads the post from LinkedIn, which sets its own cookies.
          <a href="{{ post.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it on LinkedIn instead</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}
